WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.927
Indexes are physical structures that help PostgreSQL find rows more efficiently.

00:00:04.927 --> 00:00:10.587
Primary keys and UNIQUE constraints automatically receive supporting unique indexes.

00:00:10.587 --> 00:00:14.302
Foreign-key columns on the child side are different.

00:00:14.302 --> 00:00:17.082
PostgreSQL does not automatically create every child-side index for you.

00:00:17.082 --> 00:00:23.897
Whether an index is useful depends on actual query patterns, joins, updates, deletes, and data volume.

00:00:23.897 --> 00:00:26.747
Run the provided example and inspect the indexes.

00:00:26.747 --> 00:00:31.802
At this stage, you do not need to design a full indexing strategy.

00:00:31.802 --> 00:00:36.517
The important idea is that logical relationships and physical performance structures are related but not

00:00:36.517 --> 00:00:41.557
identical. First design correct keys and constraints.

00:00:41.557 --> 00:00:44.082
Then add indexes based on real access patterns and measured performance needs.

00:00:44.082 --> 00:00:46.992
Keys are not merely technical identifiers.

00:00:46.992 --> 00:00:52.342
A primary key protects row identity, a unique constraint protects a business rule,

00:00:52.342 --> 00:00:55.702
and a foreign key protects a relationship.

00:00:55.702 --> 00:00:58.562
In production systems, surrogate keys are often convenient,

00:00:58.562 --> 00:01:03.458
but important natural identifiers may still need their own uniqueness rule.
